It is the intention of this article to adopt the minimum standards and requirements for the control of dangerous dogs and potentially dangerous dogs in compliance with the dangerous dog control law (see O.C.G.A. § 4-8-20 et seq.). Therefore, as stated by the general assembly of the state, the owner of a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog shall be solely liable for any injury to or death caused by such dog. Under no circumstances shall the county or any employee or official of the county which enforces or fails to enforce the provisions of the law or this division shall be held liable for any damages to any person who suffers an injury inflicted by:
(1) A dog that has been identified as being a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog;
(2) A dog that has been reported to the proper authority as being a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog;
(3) A dog that the county has failed to identify as a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog;
(4) A dog which has been identified as being a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog but has not been kept or restrained in the manner prescribed by law; or
(5) A dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog whose owner has not maintained insurance coverage or a surety bond as required by law.
(Code 1999, § 4-36; Ord. of 7-6-1989, § 7)

223:
(a) A dangerous dog shall be immediately confiscated by the dog control officer, by a law enforcement officer or by another person authorized by the dog control officer if the:
(1) Owner of the dog does not secure liability or bond as required by O.C.G.A. § 4-8-27(c);
(2) Dog is not validly registered as required by this division;
(3) Dog is not maintained in a proper enclosure; or
(4) Dog is outside a proper enclosure in violation of state law.
(b) A potentially dangerous dog shall be confiscated in the same manner as a dangerous dog if the dog is:
(1) Not validly registered, as required by this division and O.C.G.A. § 4-8-25;
(2) Not maintained in a proper enclosure; or
(3) Outside a proper enclosure in violation of state law.
(c) Any dog that has been confiscated pursuant to the provisions of this section shall be returned to its owner upon the owner's compliance with the provisions of this division and O.C.G.A. § 4-8-20 et seq., and upon the payment of the cost of confiscation. If the owner has not complied with the provisions of this section within 20 days of the date the dog was confiscated, such dog shall be destroyed in an expeditious and humane manner.
(d) When a dog is classified by the county animal control officer or any law enforcement agent with the power to effect arrest in the county to be a dangerous dog or a potentially dangerous dog and no person comes forward as owner of such dog and no owner, as defined by section 6-45, is otherwise readily apparent:
(1) Such dog shall be confiscated;
(2) Notice of such confiscation shall be given to all putative owners of the subject dangerous or potentially dangerous dog by posting a description of the same with a statement requiring all persons claiming the subject dog to contact the animal control officer within ten days of the date of the notice or the subject dog will be destroyed;
(3) The requisite notice shall be posted in the county courthouse in the place of posting public notices; and
(4) If no person claims any dog confiscated under this subsection or presents themselves within ten days of the posting the requisite notice, the subject dog shall be destroyed in a humane manner.
(Code 1999, § 4-43; Ord. of 7-6-1989, § 6; Ord. of 8-7-2008, § (3))

